 "source": "GLM-5.3's proof blocks (benchmarks/served_units/stage.py's types at TP8) proved on one H200 by flock-circuit prove --zk --gpu, warm, full 2^35-bit statements (benchmarks/one_stage/zk_cost.py overhead, arm dev, e2e); priced per census stratum on the census's workload card by fig1_glm.py, each stratum by the staged Definition of its kind and K (the evidence's out/figure1-glm.json names it); the strata no measured type prices (under 1% of the price) are left pending. Model h, prefill, end to end: as registered (today's circuit) dev 1.4e+10 marginal, 1.3e+11 at full statements; per expert (the design point, needs the per-expert Definitions registered) dev 4.6e+08 marginal, 1.1e+09 at full statements. The other arms: nofw (FC_HOST_WITNESS=1) 1.1e+11 marginal, 2.3e+11 at full statements registered, 2.9e+09 marginal, 3.6e+09 at full statements per expert; fw (FC_HOST_WITNESS=1 FC_FIREWALL=1, the ZK server's path; its send check fitted on drawn sessions and extrapolated to 2^35 bits) 1.7e+13 marginal, 2e+13 at full statements registered, 4.5e+11 marginal, 4.6e+11 at full statements per expert. SPOT's prover is the dev arm. Beside them, the seven block types of GLM-5.3's MoE cut per expert, under the per-expert partition card's names, so that a line on that cut prices them measured: Each per-expert block type's proving time measured on one H200 SXM (RunPod) with the deployed prover, flock-circuit prove --zk --gpu, at its partition-card statics and staged k_log, at full and half statements (2^35 witness bits at full; the k_log 25 reads also at a quarter), every block drawn, one warm process and two or three timed sessions each. Lean verity-verify-proof checked one session a type and arm at its largest statement: each passed every check, then was refused only for lacking the Program, which these statements are staged without. h is proving GPU-seconds over the MACs' seconds at 9.8943e+14 MAC/s (verity.hardware.rate, the H200's e4m3). model_overhead and the label stay the registered cut's; the per-expert cut's own is 5.53e+08 marginal end to end, 1.7e+09 at full statements. Every block's overhead is at verity.hardware.rate's peak, 9.8943e+14 MAC/s (the registered blocks' rescaled from the data sheet's 9.8945e+14). Beside them, 14 registered types of the census that no earlier card measured (the all-reduces, FlashInfer's and cuBLAS's GEMMs, the FP8 group quantization, the norms, SparseMLA's split part and combine and the residual add, under the census's names), so that the planner prices their blocks measured: each a whole Definition bound from its census name (serving_rows.template) on one bf16-row a port, staged on N(0, 1) words, its proving time measured on one H200 SXM (RunPod) with the deployed prover, flock-circuit prove --zk --gpu, at statements of 2 and 64 blocks (the planner proves one to three of these a statement, so the fixed seconds at small statements is what it charges), every block drawn, one warm process and two timed sessions each; statement_blocks is the 2^35-bit cap at the staged k_log. Lean verity-verify-proof checked the newest session of each type and arm at its largest statement. The nofw arm (FC_HOST_WITNESS=1) was measured at 2 blocks on 7 of these types, within session noise of dev, which stands in for it on the rest; fw is nofw plus the send-check law (3.02 us a slot bit once a statement, 0.0669 us a proved bit), which direct fw sessions at 2 blocks on 2 of these types put at 2.64-3.39x the law's increment: at 2 blocks their once-a-statement term is 9.39 us a slot bit, and the evidence's census.json prices fw with it beside the law. Statements of 2 and 64 blocks resolve a marginal of a few ms a block at best: 11 of these types fitted one below the earlier blocks' computed rule (their median seconds a witness bit, 1.05e-10 s, times 2^k_log), or were proved at 2 blocks only, so each takes that rule's marginal and its statement's fixed seconds from its 2-block median. Beside them, two more registered types of the census whose proving needs more host memory than that pod's 251 GB (the FP8 group quantization at K=6144 and FlashInfer's FP8 GEMM at K=2048), measured the same way on a 2x H200 SXM pod (RunPod, 503 GB of host memory), one GPU proving: the deployed prover, flock-circuit prove --zk --gpu, at statements of 2 and 64 blocks, every block drawn, one warm process and two timed sessions each, the 2^35-bit cap at the staged k_log as statement_blocks. The live verifier accepted every session; Lean verity-verify-proof checked none of these. The 2-block statement of the FP8 group quantization at K=6144 was proved again alone on the host: its first sessions ran beside another type's staging with the host's memory at its limit. FlashInfer's FP8 GEMM at K=2048 fitted a marginal below the computed rule (1.05e-10 s a witness bit, times 2^k_log), so it takes that rule's marginal and its statement's fixed seconds from its 2-block median. The FP8 group quantization at K=6144's marginal is composed from its parts: the host's growth a block (end to end less witness and proving), from statements of 2 and 64 blocks proved interleaved on one CPU node, two passes in ABBA order, warm, the statement cache on (r20261108-100959-8921: 0.28 \u00b1 0.13 ms), plus the H200 runs' device witness and proving growth a block (-2.8 \u00b1 20.5 and 8.1 \u00b1 5.8 ms). That puts it at 5.6 \u00b1 21.3 ms a block, above the computed rule and kept, with its fixed seconds from its 2-block median. The H200 runs' direct fit, 233 ms a block, was the host's: its host growth fitted 227 \u00b1 131 ms a block on that host, where the CPU node's is 0.28 ms. At K=2048, statements of 2 and 64 blocks proved alone on one H200 SXM (RunPod, 251 GB of host memory) by the same prover, at each size one cold session and one warm process of two timed sessions, the statement cache on (r20261108-120157-a741), fitted 1.38 \u00b1 0.10 ms a block end to end (its witness 0.90 and its proving 0.43 ms), below the computed rule, so it takes that rule's marginal; its fixed seconds stay its 2-block median before the statement cache, as every other entry's do (with the cache warm its 2-block sessions took 2.04 s). The same composition at K=2048 gave 7.4 \u00b1 2.6 ms: the earlier H200 runs' witness and proving growth there (6.0 and 1.3 ms) is several times this run's, so K=6144's composed marginal may read high. SparseMLA's split part at T=64 (7.7G matrix entries) and FlashInfer's FP8 GEMM at K=6144 (9.3G), whose proving needs about 760 and 920 GB of host memory at the 99 bytes an entry these two took, stay computed; plus a stand-in row for the MoE w2 variant small decode batches run (BLOCK_SIZE_M 8, BLOCK_SIZE_N 64, SWAP_AB by enable_swap_ab, MmaV2), taking the served Qgmma row's seconds: three one-block end-to-end audits of the variants on one CPU host, each accepted by the Lean verifier, are within 2% (r20261107-113657-62fd, r20261107-113603-6e9a, r20261107-110836-649b); not measured on GPU",
